    <p>Ask: why does changing the order of two addends give the same result? Why does adding ten to one addend and taking ten from the other keep the sum? Discuss before showing equations.</p>
    <h2>Activity 1 — Monuments and Falls</h2>
    <p>Use a drawing, base-ten blocks, number-line jumps or equations. The companion asks each source-context quantity separately, with its unit. The partner and teacher check the chosen representation and why the situation uses addition or subtraction.</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Iguazu Falls: combine successive drops of 115 and 131 feet.</li>
        <li>Washington Monument and Lincoln Memorial: compare heights of 555 and 99 feet.</li>
        <li>Eiffel Tower: compare 674 steps from ground to the second floor against 328 steps to the first floor.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For the steps question, compare at least two methods: counting on 328 to 674, counting back 674 to 328, or subtracting with base-ten blocks. Photographs from third-party archives are not embedded or represented as verified original source art.</p>
    <h2>Activity 2 — Journal About Connections</h2>
    <p>Write or speak about <strong>one or more</strong> of: an earlier method you recognized; a calculation you now understand well; or a remaining confusion or question. Work independently first, then share in pairs. Oral response or manipulatives are acceptable (MLR8, Action and Expression). Your teacher evaluates reasoning, not just a number.</p>
    <h2>Synthesis and materials</h2>
    <p>Explain a strategy you prefer and one you would like to learn. Use available base-ten blocks, and compare methods instead of mandating a new algorithm. Optional teacher-led centers: Target Numbers stage 6; Five in a Row stage 8; Rectangle Rumble stage 2. Source center boards are not reproduced.</p>
